a malloc-free reverse-mode autograd engine in c,
built to train karpathy's microGPT as fast as possible.

all memory is static, TigerBeetle style: fixed buffers sized at compile time,
handed out by a FixedBufferAllocator like zig's. every op output points to
the node that made it, and backward sorts that graph topologically from the
loss. each training step frees its whole graph in O(1) by resetting one
buffer. it uses float64 throughout and builds attention from the 12 primitive
ops. matmul loops are ordered for contiguous inner-loop access, and AdamW
uses sqrt instead of pow.


1000 training steps take 0.107s on one Intel Xeon Gold 6426Y core, about
107 microseconds per step for forward, backward and AdamW.


usage:

    $ make parity

    trained 1000 steps in 0.107s
    passed parity check (max abs diff 5.7e-15)
    inference (random sampled name): kaonle


see also:

- discussion on hackernews: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46285424
- counterpart in lean: https://github.com/sueszli/autograd.lean
- original microgpt implementation: https://karpathy.github.io/2026/02/12/microgpt/
- tigerstyle: https://github.com/tigerbeetle/tigerbeetle/blob/main/docs/TIGER_STYLE.md
- zig's FixedBufferAllocator: https://ziglang.org/documentation/master/std/#std.heap.FixedBufferAllocator
